        "explain": "An IPv4 address is a 32-bit numeric identifier formatted as four 8-bit octets separated by dots (e.g., `192.168.1.1`). To prevent the depletion of IPv4 addresses, RFC 1918 established three private address ranges: 1) Class A: `10.0.0.0/8`, 2) Class B: `172.16.0.0/12`, and 3) Class C: `192.168.0.0/16`. Private IPs cannot be routed across the public Internet; they require Network Address Translation (NAT) on a gateway router to translate internal source IPs into a public IP. Special ranges include `127.0.0.1` (loopback) and `169.254.0.0/16` (APIPA link-local).",
