        "explain": "A subnet mask is a 32-bit number of contiguous binary 1s followed by 0s that separates an IP address into two parts: the Network Portion (identifying the subnet) and the Host Portion (identifying the specific device). In Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR), the mask is written as a slash followed by the count of 1s (e.g., `/24` = `255.255.255.0` = 24 ones). Formula for usable hosts: $2^{(32 - \\text{prefix})} - 2$ (subtracting 2 because the all-zeros host address is the Network ID and the all-ones address is the Broadcast).",
