        "type": "fundamentals",
        "name": "02 Fundamentals",
        "explain": "Correlation is the analytical process of associating multiple independent events across disparate infrastructure systems into a single coherent incident story. An attacker enters via an ingress vector (e.g., phishing attachment on an endpoint), initiates Command and Control (C2) through an encrypted network socket, performs privilege escalation on the local OS, queries Active Directory for high-value targets, moves laterally via SMB/RDP to a database server, and exfiltrates proprietary data to an external cloud bucket. To track this, the analyst must execute an 'Entity Pivot': 1) IP -> MAC -> Hostname, 2) User SID -> Username -> Roles, 3) Process GUID -> Network Socket -> Remote IP -> DNS Query. Correlating across these disparate schemas requires standardized data models (such as Elastic Common Schema - ECS, or Splunk CIM).",
