        "type": "fundamentals",
        "name": "02 Fundamentals",
        "explain": "A forensic timeline is an unbroken, chronological ledger of events leading up to, during, and following a security incident. Building an accurate timeline requires resolving 3 technical obstacles: 1) **Time Zone Discrepancies**: Telemetry originating from servers in London (GMT), Tokyo (JST), and New York (EST) must all be converted to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). 2) **Clock Skew**: If an unmanaged switch's internal clock is 3 minutes slow because it lost contact with the corporate NTP server, all of its network flow logs will appear to happen *before* the attacker actually sent the packet. 3) **Timestamp Precision**: Modern living-off-the-land attacks execute automated PowerShell scripts within milliseconds; log parsers must preserve millisecond and microsecond timestamps (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.sssZ`).",
